1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
We've heard the warning in dozens of horror flicks.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:07,000
Don't go in the basement.

3
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:12,000
But the ghastly has become the grand.

4
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:19,000
Today, computer-aided design and robotic layout are creating a Playboy's paradise.

5
00:00:19,001 --> 00:00:22,000
The basement is a recipe for in-home pleasure.

6
00:00:22,001 --> 00:00:25,000
Just add water and watch it rise.

7
00:00:25,001 --> 00:00:29,000
It's the nerve center for climate control.

8
00:00:29,001 --> 00:00:31,000
Fire suppression.

9
00:00:31,001 --> 00:00:35,000
And it's the room most likely to save your life.

10
00:00:35,001 --> 00:00:39,000
Now, The Basement on Modern Marvels.

11
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:57,000
For centuries, basements were generally places to avoid.

12
00:00:57,001 --> 00:01:04,000
dark, dingy, consumed by moisture, draped in cobwebs, and inhabited by unwanted creatures

13
00:01:04,001 --> 00:01:07,000
and rusting relics of the past.

14
00:01:07,001 --> 00:01:11,010
Well, a basement is really a secondary space.

15
00:01:11,011 --> 00:01:16,000
It's often left unwaterproofed, unheated to some degree,

16
00:01:16,001 --> 00:01:22,000
and it has all those associations of darkness and dampness and not being pleasant.

17
00:01:22,001 --> 00:01:27,800
As ever larger suburban homes take advantage of advanced construction techniques,

18
00:01:28,340 --> 00:01:32,060
the basement is becoming as lavish and livable as the rest of the home.

19
00:01:34,700 --> 00:01:37,540
The Finished Basement Company in Denver, Colorado,

20
00:01:38,100 --> 00:01:42,020
is one of a growing number of builders specializing in the ultimate basement makeover.

21
00:01:42,940 --> 00:01:44,940
You can see this area is fairly busy.

22
00:01:45,440 --> 00:01:48,820
We've got some high-end theater equipment going in here.

23
00:01:49,280 --> 00:01:50,880
We've got a fireplace flue.

24
00:01:50,881 --> 00:01:54,100
We've got most of all our electrical and plumbing in this area.

25
00:01:54,620 --> 00:01:57,680
This area was probably the most complicated area in the basement.

26
00:01:58,160 --> 00:02:02,120
I really had to be on top of this, meet with the subcontractors on a daily basis,

27
00:02:02,520 --> 00:02:06,460
make sure everything was falling into place per plan and per code.

28
00:02:07,180 --> 00:02:12,640
The company employs 35 people and transforms more than 150 basements per year.

29
00:02:13,480 --> 00:02:17,380
It really has become a very individual expression of self,

30
00:02:17,381 --> 00:02:21,600
And so fulfilling of fantasies is really a portion of what we do.

31
00:02:22,100 --> 00:02:27,980
It's this experience of walking down, realizing, wow, this isn't a basement that I remember as a kid.

32
00:02:28,420 --> 00:02:36,040
And then having these surprises along the way that has people stop in the middle of the basement and go, wow, I can't believe this is a basement.

33
00:02:37,980 --> 00:02:41,640
Some 21st century basements are truly beyond belief.

34
00:02:41,641 --> 00:02:47,580
Sophisticated building techniques and some deep pockets will get you a basement fit for a king.

35
00:02:50,940 --> 00:02:57,540
Or in the case of this quaint Midwestern cottage, a basement roomy enough for the entire king's court.

36
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:03,980
This majestic home sports an architectural feature that's all the rage.

37
00:03:04,660 --> 00:03:06,080
The walkout basement.

38
00:03:06,081 --> 00:03:11,680
The home is built on ground that slopes from the front yard to the backyard,

39
00:03:11,681 --> 00:03:16,360
with the rear walls of the basement exposed to the world outside.

40
00:03:16,361 --> 00:03:22,880
In this case, this lower level was to be a major lifestyle part of the whole house.

41
00:03:22,881 --> 00:03:29,040
It was to be a destination that was going to be used very regularly and a favorite location to go to.

42
00:03:29,680 --> 00:03:35,460
The central core of the house does have a dramatic access from the main floor down to the lower level,

43
00:03:35,461 --> 00:03:39,880
so there's a reception area essentially that then branches out to the different functions.

44
00:03:40,740 --> 00:03:44,100
The first function is highly prized eye candy for any large basement.

45
00:03:45,580 --> 00:03:47,160
A state-of-the-art home theater.

46
00:03:48,560 --> 00:03:52,200
The lavish room sports a 120-inch projection screen,

47
00:03:53,520 --> 00:03:55,340
double-insulated sound dampening panels,

48
00:03:56,260 --> 00:03:58,940
and seating for 16 of the family's best friends.

49
00:03:59,360 --> 00:04:01,760
Like most projects, there's a lifestyle envisioned.

50
00:04:01,761 --> 00:04:07,360
You begin with all of the different functions that you'd like to have happen for your friends and your family.

51
00:04:07,740 --> 00:04:15,720
Whether there's game areas, there's spaces for the kids, there's a dream or a vision for entertaining guests or groups of people.

52
00:04:16,340 --> 00:04:19,640
When the party gets going, this basement can deliver.

53
00:04:20,680 --> 00:04:27,800
With a fully stocked temperature-controlled wine cellar and a full-service bar with built-in saltwater aquarium.

54
00:04:27,801 --> 00:04:33,540
After a night of carousing, you can get back in shape in the custom home gym.

55
00:04:35,100 --> 00:04:38,680
Or take swings in the combined batting cage and golf driving range.

56
00:04:42,060 --> 00:04:45,020
But sometimes entertaining friends is a team sport.

57
00:04:46,320 --> 00:04:50,740
Never a problem thanks to this subterranean basketball and racquetball court.

58
00:04:52,840 --> 00:04:56,460
The court is over 2,000 square feet with a 20-foot ceiling.

59
00:04:57,800 --> 00:05:10,760
Eight 700-pound retractable wall segments fold to accommodate the racquetball court.

60
00:05:10,761 --> 00:05:14,680
Basements were traditionally hideaways for men, celebrating the rituals of all things

61
00:05:14,681 --> 00:05:15,680
male.

62
00:05:15,681 --> 00:05:19,280
But the rules have changed.

63
00:05:19,281 --> 00:05:23,200
The tumult of the new century has convinced many Americans that there's no place like

64
00:05:23,201 --> 00:05:28,040
home and no place like the basement to seek refuge and recreation.

65
00:05:28,920 --> 00:05:30,400
We haven't read today.

66
00:05:30,680 --> 00:05:31,380
Uh-huh.

67
00:05:33,220 --> 00:05:36,040
But the basement means different things to different people.

68
00:05:37,040 --> 00:05:43,200
A pleasure palace, a command center for home environmental control, and even a room to

69
00:05:43,201 --> 00:05:44,280
entrust with your life.

70
00:05:46,500 --> 00:05:49,800
The basement may very well be the most important room in the house.

71
00:05:49,801 --> 00:05:54,820
just ask our ancestors from the dawn of prehistory

72
00:05:54,821 --> 00:05:59,780
who conceived the basement simply as a way to escape the temperature extremes of their environment

73
00:05:59,781 --> 00:06:07,580
subterranean levels and houses are as old as we are as a species they they go back the beginning

74
00:06:07,581 --> 00:06:12,460
when we come out of the caves and the trees when you go down three feet the temperature levels off

75
00:06:12,461 --> 00:06:18,700
to 60 degrees whatever the temperature is outside it's stable below three feet so it gives a good

76
00:06:18,701 --> 00:06:23,980
deal of protection from cold and warmth.

77
00:06:23,981 --> 00:06:29,100
In biblical times, homeowners dug deep to take advantage of another crucial feature,

78
00:06:29,101 --> 00:06:31,700
storage of perishable foods.

79
00:06:31,701 --> 00:06:38,000
Oils, fruits and grains were regularly kept in subterranean chambers.

80
00:06:38,001 --> 00:06:42,080
These ancient pantries with their cool, stable temperatures were forebearers of the latter-day

81
00:06:42,081 --> 00:06:45,580
root cellar.

82
00:06:45,581 --> 00:06:47,840
food wasn't the only item in the subterranean pantry.

83
00:06:49,020 --> 00:06:50,560
They also stored water.

84
00:06:52,220 --> 00:06:54,620
Large underground cisterns or storage wells

85
00:06:54,621 --> 00:06:57,140
captured and stored precious rainwater.

86
00:06:58,600 --> 00:07:01,200
While the ancients built these subterranean spaces

87
00:07:01,201 --> 00:07:02,340
for thousands of years,

88
00:07:02,660 --> 00:07:04,260
they were rarely beneath the home.

89
00:07:05,260 --> 00:07:07,640
The residential basement took longer to develop.

90
00:07:08,760 --> 00:07:11,360
In Greece and Rome, they were very sophisticated

91
00:07:11,361 --> 00:07:13,120
in their use of the basement.

92
00:07:13,121 --> 00:07:16,540
they used it as a heating device and a cooling device.

93
00:07:16,920 --> 00:07:21,480
The origin of modern-day climate control is evident in numerous Roman excavation sites.

94
00:07:23,540 --> 00:07:27,120
These basements contained heating elements called hypocausts.

95
00:07:29,500 --> 00:07:33,740
Hypocausts were wood-fired ovens or boilers, tended constantly by servants.

96
00:07:35,260 --> 00:07:38,580
The hot air circulated throughout the basement, heating the floor,

97
00:07:39,440 --> 00:07:43,040
then traveled upward via wall flues into the home above.

98
00:07:43,120 --> 00:07:50,360
If you look at Ostia, a Roman city, it had mainly all the amenities we have today as

99
00:07:50,361 --> 00:07:55,080
far as heating and cooling and plumbing and all the rest of it, indoor toilets and so

100
00:07:55,081 --> 00:08:00,760
on, and running water. They heated those with basement boilers.

101
00:08:00,761 --> 00:08:04,345
Some Roman basements were designed to beat the heat.

102
00:08:04,346 --> 00:08:06,620
Case in point, the 4th century AD

103
00:08:06,621 --> 00:08:10,014
settlement of Belarugia in the Tunisian desert.

104
00:08:10,015 --> 00:08:13,120
Here, wealthy homeowners constructed ornate

105
00:08:13,121 --> 00:08:16,840
subterranean chambers to take advantage of the Earth's natural cooling

106
00:08:16,841 --> 00:08:22,780
properties. Just a few feet below the desert floor, the Earth remains at a

107
00:08:22,781 --> 00:08:28,420
cooler temperature, unaffected by the hot sun. Digging a basement home could

108
00:08:28,421 --> 00:08:35,620
reduce the temperature by more than 20 degrees. The Romans also looked to the

109
00:08:35,621 --> 00:08:40,180
basement to supply their entertainment. Beneath the arena floor of the Roman

110
00:08:40,181 --> 00:08:46,720
Colosseum, a basement level with large rooms and corridors housed gladiators, slaves, and

111
00:08:46,721 --> 00:08:52,440
the wild beasts favored in the Roman games.

112
00:08:52,441 --> 00:08:56,760
The basement's role as a locale for society's subservient classes was formalized in the

113
00:08:56,761 --> 00:09:02,820
16th century by Italian architect Andrea Palladio.

114
00:09:02,821 --> 00:09:09,780
The style which still bears his name has become an architectural mainstay.

115
00:09:09,781 --> 00:09:12,000
A Palladian home was generally three floors.

116
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:15,020
An upper mezzanine floor for private quarters.

117
00:09:15,600 --> 00:09:19,360
The piano nobile level, intended for entertaining guests.

118
00:09:20,860 --> 00:09:24,120
And the basement or ground floor, containing the service quarters.

119
00:09:25,060 --> 00:09:28,400
The Palladian basement became a symbol for social standing.

120
00:09:30,800 --> 00:09:38,840
The idea of where one's places in life was also defined by this architectural space.

121
00:09:38,841 --> 00:09:45,440
People were treated as base people, and you lived either underground or on the first floor in Renaissance times.

122
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:52,520
And then the idea of the higher up you go in a piece of architecture, the closer you are to God.

123
00:09:53,740 --> 00:10:00,300
18th century Palladian designers, including Thomas Jefferson, firmly established the basement in the New World.

124
00:10:02,680 --> 00:10:06,680
Jefferson's home in Virginia, Monticello, is a true Palladian estate.

125
00:10:08,840 --> 00:10:15,240
Basements also addressed the need to squeeze additional space into overcrowded urban areas

126
00:10:15,241 --> 00:10:19,620
like 19th century New York City.

127
00:10:19,621 --> 00:10:24,000
The poverty-stricken crime-ridden neighborhood of Five Points was a notorious example of

128
00:10:24,001 --> 00:10:29,020
the urban basement as public health menace.

129
00:10:29,021 --> 00:10:33,400
Here boarding house landlords were all too eager to rent out every square foot.

130
00:10:33,401 --> 00:10:37,400
The poorest tenants were relegated to the wet, foul-smelling basements.

131
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:42,840
With a profound lack of ventilation and no way to prevent water seepage,

132
00:10:43,640 --> 00:10:46,460
these underground tenements were a breeding ground for disease.

133
00:10:47,420 --> 00:10:52,960
These people, when they came out into the daylight, had terrible, pallid skin.

134
00:10:53,120 --> 00:10:55,860
They had just been living their whole time in these underground areas.

135
00:10:56,760 --> 00:11:01,080
By the 20th century, the alarming conditions in America's urban basements

136
00:11:01,081 --> 00:11:04,020
compelled health reformers to issue a call to arms.

137
00:11:05,040 --> 00:11:08,880
One of the major themes of the modern architectural movement

138
00:11:08,881 --> 00:11:11,340
that started in the 1900s was health.

139
00:11:11,640 --> 00:11:14,200
It was a socially based movement of architecture,

140
00:11:14,201 --> 00:11:17,940
and they promoted sunshine and well-being and glass.

141
00:11:18,780 --> 00:11:22,180
And so the basement became a target because it was no sunshine

142
00:11:22,181 --> 00:11:24,400
and in many cases wet and dark.

143
00:11:26,280 --> 00:11:28,440
As the 20th century progressed,

144
00:11:28,441 --> 00:11:33,440
Better building techniques and modern materials would rescue the basement.

145
00:11:33,441 --> 00:11:41,440
By offering a cleaner, drier space, many builders would find homebuyers eager for a basement workshop and extra storage space.

146
00:11:45,440 --> 00:11:49,440
From a hole in the ground to a subterranean oasis,

147
00:11:49,441 --> 00:11:59,740
Building the basement would become a symphony of computer design, robotic layup, and some very large cake molds.

148
00:12:03,040 --> 00:12:05,900
The basement has long been a crucible for invention.

149
00:12:06,900 --> 00:12:18,140
Devices invented in basements include the vacuum cleaner, FM radio, intermittent windshield wipers, and ENIAC, widely considered to be the first electronic computer.

150
00:12:19,080 --> 00:12:21,440
The Basement will return on Modern Marvels.

151
00:12:24,700 --> 00:12:28,500
Basements are often the hippest rooms in the 21st century dream house.

152
00:12:31,960 --> 00:12:34,840
But American basements got off to a rocky start.

153
00:12:36,440 --> 00:12:39,280
In the 1800s and early 1900s,

154
00:12:39,780 --> 00:12:44,960
the best material available to construct a basement wall was rough-cut stone or brick,

155
00:12:45,520 --> 00:12:47,880
neither of which offered any waterproofing ability.

156
00:12:48,140 --> 00:12:57,880
By the 1930s, preformed concrete block, coupled with better methods for drainage, offered

157
00:12:57,881 --> 00:12:59,880
some water resistance.

158
00:12:59,881 --> 00:13:07,140
To keep up with the post-World War II housing boom, block foundations became the standard.

159
00:13:07,141 --> 00:13:13,700
But many builders in the American South and West skipped the basement entirely.

160
00:13:13,701 --> 00:13:19,448
solution was the concrete slab, a foundation built directly on the ground.

161
00:13:19,449 --> 00:13:20,380
But in other

162
00:13:20,381 --> 00:13:23,740
parts of the country, digging a basement came with the territory.

163
00:13:23,741 --> 00:13:29,660
If you take a look from a geographic standpoint where basements have been built in America,

164
00:13:29,661 --> 00:13:33,506
they tend to be in the Midwest and Northeast portion of the country.

165
00:13:33,507 --> 00:13:34,580
And there's a bunch

166
00:13:34,581 --> 00:13:37,817
of different reasons for that. Number one is you have frost.

167
00:13:37,818 --> 00:13:39,220
And so you have, you know,

168
00:13:39,221 --> 00:13:44,800
the winter, the frost will go down as low as, in some areas, 36 inches.

169
00:13:46,220 --> 00:13:50,800
Establishing the foundation below this frost line helps ensure that the house won't shift

170
00:13:50,801 --> 00:13:52,460
and cause structural damage.

171
00:13:55,860 --> 00:14:00,380
Digging a basement foundation in colder climates means excavating up to four feet

172
00:14:00,381 --> 00:14:03,920
in order to reach dry and stable soil beneath the frost line.

173
00:14:03,921 --> 00:14:11,920
Digging an additional four or more feet will accommodate a full basement.

174
00:14:11,921 --> 00:14:15,920
Concrete footings are then poured into trenches along the perimeter.

175
00:14:15,921 --> 00:14:21,920
The footings support the basement walls and ultimately the weight of the entire home.

176
00:14:21,921 --> 00:14:24,920
Many regions have different requirements for basements.

177
00:14:24,921 --> 00:14:28,920
The footings are different, the soil different, and so it requires different methods.

178
00:14:28,921 --> 00:14:30,920
So there are many different ways of doing a basement.

179
00:14:30,921 --> 00:14:37,920
One method is by which you dig out the footings, pour the footings, and then come back and pour the slab,

180
00:14:37,921 --> 00:14:39,920
which kind of makes it kind of a floating slab.

181
00:14:39,921 --> 00:14:42,920
That's generally the way in most regions it's done.

182
00:14:44,920 --> 00:14:51,920
Building today's basement foundation has evolved into a computer-controlled, moisture-resistant process.

183
00:14:51,921 --> 00:14:55,920
Often it begins with a computer-aided design, or CAD, program,

184
00:14:55,921 --> 00:14:58,920
where designers establish coordinates for the footings and walls.

185
00:14:58,921 --> 00:15:02,920
From here, the foundation is ready for layout on the property.

186
00:15:02,921 --> 00:15:05,920
Today, the layout has gone robotic.

187
00:15:05,921 --> 00:15:10,920
An innovative system called the Total Station uses a servo motor,

188
00:15:10,921 --> 00:15:17,920
infrared sensors, and radio communication to measure the elevation and horizontal axes of a foundation site.

189
00:15:20,920 --> 00:15:25,920
It reads to the prism up here and shoots an infrared beam back and forth.

190
00:15:25,921 --> 00:15:26,621
and forth.

191
00:15:26,622 --> 00:15:31,000
With the time lapse between them, it knows how far away I am for the instrument

192
00:15:31,001 --> 00:15:33,033
from the speed it gets back and forth.

193
00:15:33,034 --> 00:15:35,600
That instrument's relayed through this radio and

194
00:15:35,601 --> 00:15:37,103
the radio inside the instrument.

195
00:15:37,104 --> 00:15:39,780
And all of that's also relayed through the data collector

196
00:15:39,781 --> 00:15:43,600
that tells me where to go and my cuts and fills.

197
00:15:43,601 --> 00:15:51,971
The total station fixes coordinates accurate to the hundredth of an inch.

198
00:15:51,972 --> 00:15:53,920
Once a foundation

199
00:15:53,921 --> 00:15:59,540
is excavated and the footings are in place, the basement walls begin their upward push.

200
00:15:59,541 --> 00:16:05,900
While some builders still use the tried and true method of stacking concrete blocks, most

201
00:16:05,901 --> 00:16:08,662
have embraced the cake mold method.

202
00:16:08,663 --> 00:16:13,160
Precisely spaced panels or forms create a mold for poured

203
00:16:13,161 --> 00:16:14,160
concrete.

204
00:16:14,161 --> 00:16:19,880
In a typical basement you have a set of tall form and sometimes what we call short forms.

205
00:16:19,881 --> 00:16:23,380
Typically nowadays the tall forms are like nine foot tall.

206
00:16:23,680 --> 00:16:25,580
These aluminum forms are three foot wide.

207
00:16:26,040 --> 00:16:30,740
And a lot like a Lego set, they assemble together and are set and locked together.

208
00:16:30,920 --> 00:16:32,240
And it's a very efficient process.

209
00:16:32,460 --> 00:16:37,400
They stand them, if you will, edge to edge and assemble them right down on the footing

210
00:16:37,401 --> 00:16:42,020
and run down the wall and create a module at a time.

211
00:16:43,840 --> 00:16:46,440
Once the forms are set, the concrete is poured.

212
00:16:46,441 --> 00:16:53,440
An average of 50 cubic yards of concrete go into the basement walls of a 2,000 square foot home.

213
00:16:53,441 --> 00:16:59,440
Once the forms are removed, a perfectly flat finished wall appears.

214
00:17:03,440 --> 00:17:08,440
But there's still more to do to prevent a plague of cracks and leaking foundations.

215
00:17:08,441 --> 00:17:17,640
rubberized asphalt house wraps and other new technologies hold the promise of the bone dry

216
00:17:17,641 --> 00:17:25,680
basement the way this works is any runoff water that might come up against our basement wall

217
00:17:25,681 --> 00:17:31,060
will run down the waterproofing and then it will drain into these holes on the top of the pipe

218
00:17:31,061 --> 00:17:37,480
and drain out well this is a basement insulation that runs from the sill plate all the way to the

219
00:17:37,481 --> 00:17:41,956
footer. It's a foil scrim craft over a fiberglass bat.

220
00:17:41,957 --> 00:17:44,940
And the perforated foil craft facing

221
00:17:44,941 --> 00:17:49,880
here controls the rate at which moisture moves to the wall, but still allows drying if moisture

222
00:17:49,881 --> 00:17:52,007
comes through the foundation.

223
00:17:52,008 --> 00:17:56,480
With such efficient technology, most basement foundations are

224
00:17:56,481 --> 00:17:59,280
routine jobs for the average home builder.

225
00:17:59,281 --> 00:18:02,080
But the occasional high-dollar custom home

226
00:18:02,081 --> 00:18:05,740
is sometimes built on terrain better suited to a mountain goat.

227
00:18:07,900 --> 00:18:14,160
Each year, the Concrete Foundation Association honors builders with an award called the Basement

228
00:18:14,161 --> 00:18:14,861
of the Year.

229
00:18:15,420 --> 00:18:20,240
Because these award-winning basements generally contribute to high blood pressure, the builders

230
00:18:20,241 --> 00:18:23,740
fondly rename the award the Basement from Hell.

231
00:18:23,741 --> 00:18:31,580
Above the plains of eastern Kansas, the 2002 basement from Hell sits perched on a cliff

232
00:18:31,581 --> 00:18:33,108
of solid limestone.

233
00:18:33,109 --> 00:18:39,220
When ABI Corporation was asked to excavate a remote, multi-tiered foundation

234
00:18:39,221 --> 00:18:43,920
on solid rock, they knew the devil would have his due.

235
00:18:43,921 --> 00:18:48,460
On this particular site, we had just about everything to make this job difficult that

236
00:18:48,461 --> 00:18:49,426
as possible.

237
00:18:49,427 --> 00:18:55,460
A 39-foot elevation change, solid rock, limestone, limited access, no place

238
00:18:56,140 --> 00:18:59,338
to put excavation spoils, a very deep foundation.

239
00:18:59,339 --> 00:19:02,080
We had limited reach for our cranes to get

240
00:19:02,081 --> 00:19:04,180
our equipment in.

241
00:19:04,181 --> 00:19:10,440
Excavating the basement required a 70-ton crane and two large backhoes, one to act as

242
00:19:10,441 --> 00:19:15,700
a movable rock hammer to break up the limestone and the other to remove the debris.

243
00:19:15,701 --> 00:19:18,600
The process took about six weeks. It was very tedious.

244
00:19:18,601 --> 00:19:22,440
And as we stair-stepped this foundation in the side of this hill,

245
00:19:22,441 --> 00:19:25,700
and as we went up from one level of rock to the next,

246
00:19:25,701 --> 00:19:29,240
we could not go back down if we made a mistake.

247
00:19:29,241 --> 00:19:33,180
Inserting vertical footings and steel reinforcement at every step

248
00:19:33,181 --> 00:19:36,760
ensured that the foundation would not slip off the rock base

249
00:19:36,761 --> 00:19:41,700
as dirt was backfilled along the walls.

250
00:19:41,701 --> 00:19:45,460
Over the course of nine weeks, the basement from hell climbed the hill

251
00:19:45,461 --> 00:19:48,660
and eventually supported a home of complex beauty

252
00:19:48,661 --> 00:19:52,240
with a lower level that reinvents the traditional basement.

253
00:19:53,700 --> 00:20:00,120
It was hell for the foundation company to build it, but it's paradise to live in.

254
00:20:00,840 --> 00:20:05,000
What we wanted to do on the lower level here was to create, one, a theater,

255
00:20:05,420 --> 00:20:08,680
a place for a pool table, liquor bar,

256
00:20:08,980 --> 00:20:11,420
and then there's a couple spare bedrooms down here as well.

257
00:20:11,700 --> 00:20:17,000
It makes a real good entertaining home by using those kind of spaces as the rec room,

258
00:20:17,620 --> 00:20:21,060
but with the tall ceilings and all the light towards the south,

259
00:20:21,320 --> 00:20:25,700
it's the rec room that becomes livable instead of the dark, dingy basement.

260
00:20:26,560 --> 00:20:31,000
For many homeowners, a basement built to impress the neighbors is all that matters.

261
00:20:32,000 --> 00:20:34,640
But for homeowners inside the tornado belt,

262
00:20:35,440 --> 00:20:39,420
a basement may be the only thing between them and disaster.

263
00:20:41,700 --> 00:20:50,080
According to the 2003 International Residential Code, basement bedrooms must include an escape

264
00:20:50,081 --> 00:20:56,080
or egress window, with the sill no more than 44 inches off the floor.

265
00:20:56,081 --> 00:21:06,920
The basement will return on Modern Marvels.

266
00:21:06,921 --> 00:21:10,420
Over time, the modern basement has improved its image.

267
00:21:10,421 --> 00:21:16,920
From a dingy storeroom to a high-tech pleasure palace, basements are now considered valuable

268
00:21:16,921 --> 00:21:17,920
real estate.

269
00:21:17,921 --> 00:21:23,800
But even as they've helped homeowners improve the quality of their lives, some have also

270
00:21:23,801 --> 00:21:29,600
helped to save them.

271
00:21:29,601 --> 00:21:35,840
By the end of World War II and the beginning of the atomic age, few Americans could foresee

272
00:21:35,841 --> 00:21:42,340
how close the threat of nuclear holocaust could get to their own living rooms.

273
00:21:42,341 --> 00:21:48,080
As the escalating Cold War took the superpowers near the brink, some Americans became convinced

274
00:21:48,081 --> 00:21:52,220
that steps had to be taken to ensure survival.

275
00:21:52,221 --> 00:21:57,260
In 1961, after the Cuban Missile Crisis and the Berlin Wall speech, President Kennedy

276
00:21:57,261 --> 00:22:02,840
addressed the nation in May of 61 and urged citizens to prepare for the unlikely event

277
00:22:02,841 --> 00:22:07,280
but the possibility of an attack on the United States by another nation,

278
00:22:07,880 --> 00:22:12,860
and directed the Department of Defense to begin a survey of basements

279
00:22:12,861 --> 00:22:14,920
in large buildings throughout the United States

280
00:22:14,921 --> 00:22:17,220
that could be used as shelters in the event of an attack.

281
00:22:17,880 --> 00:22:20,940
While thousands of public fallout shelters were constructed

282
00:22:20,941 --> 00:22:23,020
in the basements of existing public buildings,

283
00:22:23,500 --> 00:22:26,160
many citizens wanted something closer to home.

284
00:22:27,480 --> 00:22:30,960
People sometimes put a shelter inside their basement.

285
00:22:30,961 --> 00:22:41,105
They would partition off a part of the basement and construct a fallout shelter to the design criteria that the government had issued as part of the 1960s effort to

286
00:22:41,106 --> 00:22:42,280
prepare the nation.

287
00:22:43,340 --> 00:22:47,960
For those willing to swallow the sales pitch, the basement was an antidote to Armageddon.

288
00:22:49,040 --> 00:22:52,097
Well, folks, I'm glad you could come down to see my fallout shelter.

289
00:22:52,098 --> 00:22:53,760
Just finished painting it last night.

290
00:22:54,580 --> 00:22:56,360
Looks like a nice job, Wal.

291
00:22:56,361 --> 00:23:00,700
Well, you know, this shelter is a real good idea.

292
00:23:01,360 --> 00:23:05,260
If we should ever have a nuclear war, we could get a heavy fallout,

293
00:23:05,320 --> 00:23:08,120
even though we were not anywhere near the target area.

294
00:23:08,520 --> 00:23:11,900
They were mostly trying to protect themselves from the effect of gamma radiation.

295
00:23:12,680 --> 00:23:16,640
After a nuclear attack, there would be a large amount of nuclear dust,

296
00:23:17,040 --> 00:23:18,800
and as that dust settled on the ground,

297
00:23:19,300 --> 00:23:23,780
that gamma radiation would give off life-threatening levels of high radiation

298
00:23:23,781 --> 00:23:25,900
for a period of about two weeks.

299
00:23:26,360 --> 00:23:28,760
Hey, isn't this nice?

300
00:23:29,920 --> 00:23:34,320
Well, Sir Ruth and I certainly can live in here very comfortably for at least two weeks.

301
00:23:35,400 --> 00:23:39,320
Although America's basement fallout shelters were never put to the real test,

302
00:23:40,100 --> 00:23:43,720
some did protect thousands from a natural threat, no less frightening.

303
00:23:48,000 --> 00:23:51,380
The tornado belt is a large portion of the United States,

304
00:23:51,980 --> 00:23:54,780
extending from southern Texas into all areas of the Midwest.

305
00:23:56,360 --> 00:23:59,120
These freak storms defy description.

306
00:23:59,700 --> 00:24:04,460
When you talk to tornado victims, they talk about the fear of going through a tornado.

307
00:24:04,960 --> 00:24:09,920
And they talk about that when there are subsequent tornado watches or warnings,

308
00:24:10,440 --> 00:24:13,800
the uncontrollable fear that they have that they have no place to seek refuge.

309
00:24:15,380 --> 00:24:18,880
But many have taken refuge from tornadoes in basements.

310
00:24:21,000 --> 00:24:25,020
Seeking shelter below ground offers some protection against these destructive storms.

311
00:24:26,360 --> 00:24:32,820
Basement safety took a quantum leap forward in 1970, in the aftermath of a catastrophe in Texas.

312
00:24:34,760 --> 00:24:42,740
On May 11, 1970, the strongest-rated tornado event, a Force 5, struck the town of Lubbock, Texas.

313
00:24:43,720 --> 00:24:47,200
Twenty people were killed, and the city was devastated.

314
00:24:47,201 --> 00:24:55,040
In the following days, the Civil Engineering Department at Texas Tech University in Lubbock

315
00:24:55,041 --> 00:24:59,500
practically invented the field of tornado impact science.

316
00:24:59,501 --> 00:25:04,180
We saw in the Lubbock tornado laboratory that was made for us.

317
00:25:04,181 --> 00:25:09,980
We had some young faculty who were poised to do research and simply took cameras in

318
00:25:09,981 --> 00:25:16,960
hand and went out in the field and did an extensive documentation of that storm.

319
00:25:16,961 --> 00:25:21,880
The result is the Wind Engineering Research Center at Texas Tech.

320
00:25:21,881 --> 00:25:28,280
The facility is dedicated to teaching builders how to erect safer homes and buildings, including

321
00:25:28,281 --> 00:25:32,020
the basement safety zone.

322
00:25:32,021 --> 00:25:37,000
At the heart of the center is the debris cannon, a high-powered simulator that fires missiles

323
00:25:37,001 --> 00:25:43,000
of common building materials at the velocity of flying debris in a Force V tornado.

324
00:25:43,001 --> 00:25:50,000
The debris cannon employs an air pressurized 22-foot barrel to fire missiles as large as

325
00:25:50,001 --> 00:25:56,300
2 by 6 boards, up to 200 miles per hour.

326
00:25:56,301 --> 00:26:00,820
When flung by an actual tornado, this common building material packs the penetrating force

327
00:26:00,821 --> 00:26:05,920
of an artillery shell.

328
00:26:05,921 --> 00:26:10,540
The key to any basement in Tornado Alley is to protect its occupants from these deadly

329
00:26:10,541 --> 00:26:11,617
projectiles.

330
00:26:11,618 --> 00:26:19,160
If the basement has a concrete roof on it that will withstand the debris impacts and

331
00:26:19,161 --> 00:26:24,042
not lift off in a storm, then it provides generally good protection.

332
00:26:24,043 --> 00:26:26,340
We still must have a door system

333
00:26:26,341 --> 00:26:32,620
on there because one would not want to be in even a basement if the door was blown off and debris

334
00:26:32,621 --> 00:26:35,379
could enter or other things could enter.

335
00:26:35,380 --> 00:26:39,380
Chiefly constructed doors are common in rural areas of the

336
00:26:39,381 --> 00:26:40,242
tornado belt.

337
00:26:40,243 --> 00:26:45,280
The debris cannon demonstrates that many doors offer little or no protection

338
00:26:45,281 --> 00:26:47,200
from tornado-driven projectiles.

339
00:26:51,200 --> 00:26:57,060
The things we learned quickly is that there's a lot of energy in this missile traveling at

340
00:26:57,061 --> 00:27:03,900
that speed, and it requires a very hardened and tough section to withstand this.

341
00:27:03,901 --> 00:27:11,900
According to the Federal Emergency Management Agency, or FEMA, basements offer some protection,

342
00:27:11,901 --> 00:27:15,900
but not enough to stop falling debris if the home is ripped from the foundation.

343
00:27:19,900 --> 00:27:24,900
The only way to beat the odds is a separately constructed safe room shelter.

344
00:27:24,901 --> 00:27:30,900
Most shelters that are built in basements normally make use of existing walls,

345
00:27:30,901 --> 00:27:34,700
and to reduce the cost, you'd probably put the shelter in a corner

346
00:27:34,701 --> 00:27:38,400
so you could make use of two walls of the basement.

347
00:27:38,880 --> 00:27:42,460
The key thing is that you would have to provide extra reinforcement.

348
00:27:44,520 --> 00:27:48,180
These basement safe rooms make thousands of Americans rest easier

349
00:27:48,181 --> 00:27:49,800
when a tornado warning goes out.

350
00:27:51,220 --> 00:27:54,380
But what happens to the millions of homeowners who don't have a basement?

351
00:27:55,960 --> 00:27:59,100
For them, FEMA and Texas Tech University

352
00:27:59,101 --> 00:28:04,840
have designed the first floor safe room, offering all the security of a basement without the

353
00:28:04,841 --> 00:28:06,700
trip downstairs.

354
00:28:06,701 --> 00:28:11,820
When you get above ground, you have to worry about the lateral loads from the wind and

355
00:28:11,821 --> 00:28:16,660
the missiles, as we call them, which is the airborne debris, striking the shelter, striking

356
00:28:16,661 --> 00:28:21,140
the wall, striking the door of the shelter that could punch through and damage the structure

357
00:28:21,141 --> 00:28:25,940
and injure people inside the shelter.

358
00:28:25,941 --> 00:28:31,060
To resist deadly debris and winds up to 200 miles per hour, these above-ground shelters

359
00:28:31,061 --> 00:28:34,700
are built for physical abuse.

360
00:28:34,701 --> 00:28:39,240
Typically they are built toward the center of the floor plan, creating a pantry or a

361
00:28:39,241 --> 00:28:42,280
walk-in closet on steroids.

362
00:28:42,281 --> 00:28:44,480
The door is the weakest link in a safe room.

363
00:28:44,481 --> 00:28:48,340
It's very important that the door has six points of connection to avoid having missiles

364
00:28:48,341 --> 00:28:49,940
penetrate the door.

365
00:28:49,941 --> 00:28:51,340
You have six points of connection here.

366
00:28:51,341 --> 00:28:53,520
You have three safe bolts on this end.

367
00:28:53,521 --> 00:28:59,020
You have three hinges on this end, plus the door is thickened steel so that the missiles won't puncture through it.

368
00:28:59,340 --> 00:29:03,540
If you don't have the six points of connection, the door can actually bend in and missiles can come in.

369
00:29:04,300 --> 00:29:08,440
The walls themselves, in this case, are insulated concrete form design.

370
00:29:08,960 --> 00:29:13,380
Those design include reinforcing steel and then concrete's poured inside the blocks,

371
00:29:13,520 --> 00:29:15,880
and you wind up with a concrete reinforced wall.

372
00:29:16,080 --> 00:29:17,840
The ceiling's built the same way.

373
00:29:18,520 --> 00:29:21,080
Foam blocks are laid across the ceiling with reinforcing steel,

374
00:29:21,081 --> 00:29:25,860
and then concrete is poured over the top to avoid having the free-falling missiles puncture the ceiling.

375
00:29:27,260 --> 00:29:29,900
While first-floor shelters will save lives,

376
00:29:30,920 --> 00:29:35,680
ultimately an approved safe room built in the basement offers the greatest possible protection.

377
00:29:38,580 --> 00:29:42,320
But protecting occupants from tornadoes isn't all that a basement can do.

378
00:29:43,620 --> 00:29:49,520
These underground spaces can now fight fires and sanitize the air that we breathe.

379
00:29:51,080 --> 00:30:02,240
The 1959 Civil Defense booklet, The Family Fallout Shelter, recommended that sanitation supplies include one quart of a solution containing DDT.

380
00:30:03,000 --> 00:30:05,860
The Basement will return on Modern Marvels.

381
00:30:08,800 --> 00:30:09,920
Thank you.

382
00:30:10,600 --> 00:30:11,820
Second, third.

383
00:30:12,200 --> 00:30:15,900
This basement in Kansas City, Missouri is designed to be the life of the party.

384
00:30:15,901 --> 00:30:24,040
It entertains, offers an escape from the world above, and keeps the family in good health.

385
00:30:24,680 --> 00:30:30,220
But for the average basement, the first sworn duty is keeping the rest of the home warm and toasty.

386
00:30:32,040 --> 00:30:39,980
In the early part of the 20th century, many homeowners gave up the wood-burning stove and switched to the coal-fired furnace.

387
00:30:39,981 --> 00:30:46,060
Each week, loads of raw coal were unloaded down a chute through a window and into the basement.

388
00:30:47,240 --> 00:30:55,380
At the turn of the century, that would be shoveled in the morning and the fire would be stoked and that would operate partway through the day.

389
00:30:55,840 --> 00:31:00,080
Probably stoke at about dinner time and then just before bed, go down and shovel coal in again.

390
00:31:00,800 --> 00:31:07,760
Literally, the furnace would heat the air and by gravity or buoyancy, the air would move up through the house.

391
00:31:07,761 --> 00:31:10,480
and houses at that time didn't really have duct work.

392
00:31:12,440 --> 00:31:17,360
Over the decades, coal was replaced by other fossil fuels like oil and gas.

393
00:31:18,320 --> 00:31:23,300
The technology changed, but the inefficiency and emissions of the gravity systems

394
00:31:23,301 --> 00:31:26,280
kept the basement a sooty, foul-smelling room.

395
00:31:26,920 --> 00:31:30,040
Gravity systems didn't even have ducts. They were just holes in the floors.

396
00:31:30,420 --> 00:31:33,920
We got a little bit more efficient, a little bit better at distributing air.

397
00:31:34,140 --> 00:31:36,960
We ended up going to sheet metal ducts like we see here.

398
00:31:37,760 --> 00:31:44,760
Take a peek into today's basements and you'll find a new wave of climate control.

399
00:31:44,761 --> 00:31:51,760
From electric furnaces and filters to ventilators and even fire suppression systems.

400
00:31:51,761 --> 00:31:55,760
But some basements are taking climate control to a whole new level.

401
00:31:55,761 --> 00:32:01,760
In the Berkshire mountains of western Massachusetts, a residence called the New American Castle

402
00:32:01,761 --> 00:32:06,620
is an ultra-modern rethinking of a historically energy inefficient structure.

403
00:32:08,300 --> 00:32:12,360
While many castles sported a dungeon, complete with torture devices,

404
00:32:13,140 --> 00:32:17,720
this version employs a basement equipped with the latest in home comfort and safety systems.

405
00:32:20,260 --> 00:32:25,580
When the castle's completed, we'll have a castle that has the best in old world styling

406
00:32:25,581 --> 00:32:27,260
and the most modern technologies.

407
00:32:27,420 --> 00:32:29,680
It'll be very kind to the environment.

408
00:32:29,681 --> 00:32:32,500
It'll use very little fuel, very little electricity.

409
00:32:33,180 --> 00:32:35,640
Since we use very little fuel, we have very little emissions.

410
00:32:35,880 --> 00:32:38,600
Everything is very clean. Everything is very comfortable.

411
00:32:40,160 --> 00:32:43,860
Comfort begins with keeping warm during Massachusetts' long winters.

412
00:32:44,640 --> 00:32:47,520
But you won't find heated air blowing through vents here.

413
00:32:48,460 --> 00:32:50,560
The castle is warmed by radiant heat.

414
00:32:52,400 --> 00:32:54,540
A boiler in the basement heats water,

415
00:32:55,060 --> 00:32:58,500
which is then circulated through tubing embedded in the floors of the castle.

416
00:32:59,300 --> 00:33:02,100
The heat radiates through the floors and into the home.

417
00:33:03,880 --> 00:33:05,920
During construction of the new American castle,

418
00:33:06,440 --> 00:33:11,980
workers routed more than 16,000 feet of half-inch tubing into the concrete floors on each level.

419
00:33:13,460 --> 00:33:14,580
There's a lot of concrete.

420
00:33:15,060 --> 00:33:17,000
We've got very good insulation in this building.

421
00:33:17,380 --> 00:33:20,380
And we're heating the objects and not directly the air.

422
00:33:21,000 --> 00:33:23,160
We want to modulate that water temperature,

423
00:33:23,280 --> 00:33:26,960
and we modulate it by having sensors outside that detect the air temperature

424
00:33:26,961 --> 00:33:29,140
and the warm water circulating through the floor.

425
00:33:29,640 --> 00:33:32,020
And through some programming, we can change that temperature

426
00:33:32,021 --> 00:33:36,240
to automatically increase or decrease as it gets warmer or colder outside.

427
00:33:39,260 --> 00:33:42,560
There is one high-efficiency heating technology, however,

428
00:33:42,700 --> 00:33:46,780
that taps energy from the same source that keeps basement temperatures stable.

429
00:33:48,540 --> 00:33:50,760
It's called the geothermal heat pump.

430
00:33:51,580 --> 00:33:53,720
Heat pumps operate on Earth power.

431
00:33:54,680 --> 00:33:57,840
The Earth absorbs and stores the sun's energy as heat.

432
00:33:58,880 --> 00:34:02,000
Underground temperatures remain within a narrow range year-round.

433
00:34:03,680 --> 00:34:10,180
Generally located in the basement, the geothermal heat pump taps this reliable energy source through a heat exchanger

434
00:34:10,181 --> 00:34:13,220
or a system of tubing buried outside the basement.

435
00:34:14,400 --> 00:34:19,840
With geothermal, with the constant temperature underground, you're able to use that by extracting heat from it

436
00:34:19,841 --> 00:34:23,360
using pipe, high-density polyethylene pipe that we put in the ground.

437
00:34:23,720 --> 00:34:28,320
You run water in it or mix your water in any freeze, and you're able to heat or cool your home.

438
00:34:29,300 --> 00:34:34,340
When surface area is limited, the tube can also be sunk in wells vertically below the home.

439
00:34:35,280 --> 00:34:40,320
Or to maximize ground space, the pipes can be coiled in a slinky pattern and buried.

440
00:34:42,820 --> 00:34:45,200
While heating and cooling is job number one,

441
00:34:45,500 --> 00:34:49,720
today the basement is also ventilating and purifying the air that we breathe.

442
00:34:49,721 --> 00:34:56,780
And ventilation's function really is to dilute the pollutants that are inside the occupied space.

443
00:34:56,781 --> 00:34:59,380
And the pollutants aren't necessarily bad things. It's moisture.

444
00:35:00,080 --> 00:35:04,340
It's the fact that we breathe, we put out carbon dioxide, and that needs to be diluted.

445
00:35:04,800 --> 00:35:07,680
We cook and create odors and smells in the house.

446
00:35:07,800 --> 00:35:09,600
We take showers and we create moisture.

447
00:35:09,860 --> 00:35:14,720
All of those things need to be reduced in their level to provide a good interior environment.

448
00:35:14,721 --> 00:35:22,700
The heat recovery ventilator, or HRV, is becoming a common basement appliance.

449
00:35:23,540 --> 00:35:28,760
It uses the heating and cooling ducts to exhaust the stale air and introduce the fresh air,

450
00:35:29,280 --> 00:35:31,520
while maintaining a constant temperature in the home.

451
00:35:33,580 --> 00:35:39,520
The fresh air stream passes through a heat exchange unit, made of multiple plates of aluminum or plastic.

452
00:35:40,500 --> 00:35:46,040
At the same time, the exhaust of stale indoor air passes through the fresh air path at an angle.

453
00:35:48,540 --> 00:35:52,960
Heat from the outgoing air is exchanged and passed back into the home with the fresh air.

454
00:35:55,000 --> 00:35:59,540
So it reduces the amount of heat that the space conditioning system has to add in the winter

455
00:35:59,541 --> 00:36:03,940
and reduces the amount of cooling that the space conditioning system has to add in the summer.

456
00:36:04,560 --> 00:36:09,040
Clean air is a nice touch, but some basements play a higher stakes game.

457
00:36:09,520 --> 00:36:15,280
For homeowners with too much to lose, there's a new high-tech sentry in the basement, the

458
00:36:15,281 --> 00:36:18,680
